{:edb/db-before-t 1504 :edb/db-after-t 1505 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534817 50 #inst "2026-10-12T16:18:31.807Z" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186129275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436974 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186278366 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186278366 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 false] [17592186394497 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186394497 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 false] [17592186394504 1071 "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186394504 1071 "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 false] [17592186394504 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2d/2d50f2375c071d8bfb4c5c58a836bef76a70557c2593d4df9acf48f9f0e7527b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/98/98e9c8442c17cbc77083c5dc55a9d74324befa05e3cd47e1c65c38cd510c08c6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186394504 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/fe/fe82880fec605db684a2b5cb0da5c88a38da5b4fb4faad1f80c4d5b0691fde21.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/9e/9e1693005c8930a7b2b8a3b47bc96f5441bf72ee6c5b67f3b8c97d87cfa2e34b.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 false] [17592186436967 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436975 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436976 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436977 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436978 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436979 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1045 "Double Integrals in Plane Polar Coordinates" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1046 #edb/ref 17592186436977 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049084 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436967 1053 #uuid "ca073d0c-56ea-5b26-866f-40f41bef64a7" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436968 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436968 1082 #edb/ref 1759218604