417 true] [17592186436973 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436973 1077 "selection" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436973 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436970 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436973 1079 #uuid "2e485133-85f6-59c3-b320-b44902b78672" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436973 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436974 1069 "q-161064" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436974 1071 "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A=$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436974 1072 #uuid "0f9560d7-409d-5ebf-b57a-a19bcb1f8e6e" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436974 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0a5627965f71f6eff869a45b1b575b892a67ebba9b1ec969f5dc38ba84cb250.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(0,-3).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/75/7515fffda1580baa332df454e7fe53a60f70944215f13d83e0e2269a0101dba6.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =2\\pi $ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436975 1062 #uuid "3ce42576-d948-5b3a-bad1-7635ed78b247" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436975 1063 #edb/ref 17592186436974 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436976 1058 #edb/ref 17592186436979 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436976 1062 #uuid "6713b327-93ce-5c20-b7f6-32726c7bfe62" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436976 1063 #edb/ref 17592186351516 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436977 1058 #edb/ref 17592186436978 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436977 1062 #uuid "774aa604-337e-5fae-94a6-366f24c2837b" 13194139534817 17592186045417 true] [17592186436977 106